<p>Then sign in with a ChatGPT, Claude, Grok, or Muse Code account, or paste an API key. A
five-step tutorial walks you through the rest.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[!NOTE]
The Windows installer and app are code-signed, and the Mac app is signed and
notarized. If SmartScreen still appears, choose <strong>More info → Run anyway</strong>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Or run it in the terminal (Node.js 22.19+ or 24+):</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">npm install -g mixdog
mixdog
</code></pre>
<details>
<summary>Supported providers</summary>

<ul>
<li>Anthropic API keys and Claude account OAuth</li>
<li>OpenAI API keys and ChatGPT/Codex account OAuth</li>
<li>Google Gemini API keys</li>
<li>xAI API keys and Grok account OAuth</li>
<li>Muse Code (Meta) account OAuth</li>
<li>OpenRouter, DeepSeek, and OpenCode Go</li>
<li>Built-in <strong>Local Provider</strong> — download and run models in the app
(Windows x64 with an NVIDIA GPU)</li>
</ul>
<p>Cursor, Antigravity (Gemini), and MiMo OAuth are off by default under
<strong>Settings → Developer</strong>; using them through OAuth risks account restrictions.</p>
</details>

<details>
<summary>Headless <code>mixdog exec</code></summary>

<p><code>mixdog exec</code> runs one non-interactive, single-model session without personal
memory, prior sessions, skills, MCP servers, or plugins:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">mixdog exec --provider openai-oauth --model gpt-5.6-sol --effort xhigh &quot;fix the failing test&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Web search is off by default (<code>--web-search</code> enables it). This does not block
shell networking — headless exec is not an offline sandbox. Run
<code>mixdog --help</code> for the full reference.</p>
</details>

<h2>Benchmarks</h2>
<p align="center">
  <picture>
    <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" srcset="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tribgames/mixdog/main/design/promo/tb21-sol-vs-codex-dark.svg">
    <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" srcset="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tribgames/mixdog/main/design/promo/tb21-sol-vs-codex-light.svg">
    <img alt="Bar chart: Mixdog vs Codex CLI on Terminal-Bench 2.1" src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tribgames/mixdog/main/design/promo/tb21-sol-vs-codex-light.svg">
  </picture>
</p>

<p align="center">
  <i>All 89 Terminal-Bench 2.1 tasks × 5 trials on GPT-5.6 Sol xhigh, scored by the official Harbor verifier.</i>
</p>

<p>Mixdog used fewer tokens on 87 of 89 tasks. With Claude Opus 5, it solved 2
more tasks than Claude Code at 19% lower cost. Every verdict, log, and script
is published in <a href="benchmarks/terminal-bench-2.1/"><code>benchmarks/terminal-bench-2.1/</code></a>,
and <a href="docs/context-efficiency.md">Context efficiency</a> explains where the
savings come from.</p>
<details>
<summary><b>Benchmark history</b></summary>

<p>Newest first. Each run links to its details, records, and caveats.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Run</th>
<th>Model · trials</th>
<th>vs</th>
<th>Score</th>
<th>Cost</th>
<th>Final context</th>
<th>Time / trial</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><a href="benchmarks/terminal-bench-2.1/#bench-log">2026-10-15</a></td>
<td>GPT-6.1 Sol xhigh · <code>k=1</code></td>
<td>Codex CLI</td>
<td>77 vs 78 /89</td>
<td><strong>−55%</strong></td>
<td><strong>−68%</strong> (10.8K vs 33.6K)</td>
<td>344s vs 380s</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><a href="benchmarks/terminal-bench-2.1/#gpt-56-sol-xhigh-vs-codex-cli">2026-08-25</a></td>
